CAT = "rdFoodSci"; SEG = "FSC"
Q = [
("Starch granules swell and thicken a sauce as it is heated in liquid. What is this process called?",
 "Gelatinization", ["Retrogradation", "Caramelization", "Denaturation"],
 "Gelatinization is the swelling of starch granules with water and heat, which thickens the liquid. Retrogradation is the later re-forming of starch crystals that causes staling and weeping."),
("A seared steak browns because amino acids react with reducing sugars. What is this reaction?",
 "Maillard reaction", ["Caramelization", "Enzymatic browning", "Hydrogenation"],
 "The Maillard reaction between amino groups and reducing sugars creates the browned color and flavor of seared meat and baked crusts. Caramelization involves sugar alone at higher heat."),
("Sliced apples turn brown on standing. What prevents this best?",
 "Dipping in lemon juice", ["Storing them at room heat", "Sprinkling them with salt water", "Cutting them with a dull knife"],
 "Browning is caused by polyphenol oxidase. Acid and ascorbic acid in lemon juice lower the pH and slow the enzyme; cutting with a dull knife damages more cells and speeds browning."),
("Which ingredient in mayonnaise acts as the emulsifier?",
 "Lecithin in the egg yolk", ["Acetic acid in vinegar", "Sodium in the salt", "Water in the lemon juice"],
 "Egg yolk lecithin has water-loving and fat-loving ends that hold oil droplets in the water phase. Vinegar adds flavor and acidity but does not emulsify."),
("Why does bread flour make a chewier product than cake flour?",
 "More protein for gluten", ["More starch for softness", "Less protein, more sugar", "A higher fat content"],
 "Bread flour is higher in the proteins glutenin and gliadin, which form a strong, elastic gluten network when kneaded. Cake flour is low in protein for a tender crumb."),
("A muffin recipe using baking soda turns out bitter and soapy. What is the likely cause?",
 "Too little acid in the batter", ["Too much flour stirred in", "Oven heat set much too low", "Too little salt added"],
 "Baking soda needs an acid (buttermilk, yogurt, brown sugar) to react. Without enough acid, excess soda leaves a soapy, bitter taste and a yellow color."),
("Adding a pinch of cream of tartar to egg whites before whipping does what?",
 "Stabilizes the foam", ["Speeds up the yolk setting", "Adds sweetness", "Lowers the volume"],
 "The acid in cream of tartar strengthens the protein film so the foam is more stable and less likely to weep. Even a trace of fat from yolk prevents a good foam."),
("Green beans cooked a long time with a lid on turn olive-drab. Why?",
 "Acids turn chlorophyll drab", ["Heat turns chlorophyll red", "Steam adds more green color", "Salt turns beans brown"],
 "Plant acids released during cooking collect under a lid and convert chlorophyll to olive-brown pheophytin. Short cooking, uncovered at first, keeps the bright green."),
("Which cooking method best keeps water-soluble vitamins in vegetables?",
 "Steaming for a short time", ["Boiling in plenty of water", "Long simmering in soup", "Soaking cut pieces overnight"],
 "Water-soluble vitamins such as vitamin C and folate leach into cooking water and break down with heat. Short cooking in little or no water, as with steaming, keeps more of them."),
("Panelists are given three samples, two alike and one different, and asked to pick the odd one. Which sensory test is this?",
 "Triangle test", ["Hedonic scale", "Paired preference", "Ranking test"],
 "The triangle test is a difference test showing whether people can tell two products apart. A hedonic scale measures how much people like a product."),
("A label says a vitamin D supplement 'supports bone health' with an FDA disclaimer. What type of statement is this?",
 "Structure/function claim", ["Authorized health claim", "Nutrient content claim", "Qualified health claim"],
 "A structure/function claim describes a role in normal body function and, on supplements, carries the disclaimer that the FDA has not evaluated it. Health claims link a substance to reducing disease risk."),
("Which phytochemical gives tomatoes their red color and is better absorbed from cooked tomatoes?",
 "Lycopene", ["Lutein", "Anthocyanin", "Resveratrol"],
 "Lycopene is a fat-soluble carotenoid; cooking and a little fat release it from the plant matrix. Lutein is found in leafy greens and egg yolks, and anthocyanins in berries."),
]
